1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,066
This is a practical walkthrough of Get-ExoQueue.

2
00:00:03,066 --> 00:00:08,368
Everything you are about to see is a real run against a real tenant, not a mock-up.

3
00:00:08,368 --> 00:00:11,178
One thing to be clear about before we start.

4
00:00:11,178 --> 00:00:14,372
Exchange Online does not expose a transport queue.

5
00:00:14,372 --> 00:00:19,993
There is no queue object to read, so this tool approximates one from message trace data.

6
00:00:19,993 --> 00:00:28,488
That approximation is genuinely useful, but please repeat that caveat to customers, because a message trace row is not a queue entry.

7
00:00:29,088 --> 00:00:30,598
Step one, load the tool.

8
00:00:30,598 --> 00:00:35,256
Get-ExoQueue is a function in a script file, so you have to dot-source it.

9
00:00:35,256 --> 00:00:37,962
That is a dot, then a space, then the path.

10
00:00:37,962 --> 00:00:49,164
If you run the file normally instead, the function is defined inside a scope that is thrown away the moment the file finishes, so nothing is loaded and nothing appears to happen.

11
00:00:49,164 --> 00:00:55,080
The script notices when you have done that and tells you, which saves a confusing few minutes.

12
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:58,001
Step two, connect to Exchange Online.

13
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:03,457
You need the Exchange Online Management module, and a role that can read message trace.

14
00:01:03,457 --> 00:01:05,402
View-Only Recipients is enough.

15
00:01:05,402 --> 00:01:09,290
If you are already connected, the tool just uses that session.

16
00:01:09,290 --> 00:01:12,113
If you are not, it offers to connect for you.

17
00:01:12,113 --> 00:01:14,308
The exception is an unattended run.

18
00:01:14,308 --> 00:01:23,904
Under quiet mode the tool cannot show you a prompt, so connect first and pass the force switch, or it will stop waiting for an answer you will never see.

19
00:01:24,504 --> 00:01:25,731
Step three, run it.

20
00:01:25,731 --> 00:01:30,830
Here is a real run against a lab tenant, asking for the last seventy two hours.

21
00:01:30,830 --> 00:01:32,444
Notice the status filter.

22
00:01:32,444 --> 00:01:42,386
Get me into the habit early, because it is the single most common source of confusion with this tool: the status parameter defaults to Pending on its own.

23
00:01:42,386 --> 00:01:50,713
A tenant full of failed or delivered mail will report zero, correctly, and that looks exactly like an empty queue when it is not.

24
00:01:50,713 --> 00:01:54,264
Naming the statuses you want removes all of that doubt.

25
00:01:54,864 --> 00:01:56,967
Step four, reading what came back.

26
00:01:56,967 --> 00:02:00,122
There are four things on this screen worth knowing.

27
00:02:00,122 --> 00:02:01,545
The first is the count.

28
00:02:01,545 --> 00:02:06,804
Fifty two messages, and in brackets, one hundred and fifty four recipient deliveries.

29
00:02:06,804 --> 00:02:08,660
Those are different questions.

30
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:16,702
One message to a distribution list is one message but many deliveries, so conflating them makes a queue look far worse than it is.

31
00:02:16,702 --> 00:02:19,362
Quote the message count as the queue depth.

32
00:02:19,362 --> 00:02:20,847
The second is queue age.

33
00:02:20,847 --> 00:02:22,950
This matters as much as the depth.

34
00:02:22,950 --> 00:02:27,961
A hundred thousand messages thirty seconds old is a burst that will clear itself.

35
00:02:27,961 --> 00:02:31,240
The same hundred thousand six hours old is an outage.

36
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:33,900
The count on its own cannot tell you which.

37
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:36,313
The third is the destination breakdown.

38
00:02:36,313 --> 00:02:41,633
Exchange Online has no real next hop domain, so the recipient domain stands in for it.

39
00:02:41,633 --> 00:02:46,396
When one destination is deferring, its domain climbs to the top of that list.

40
00:02:46,396 --> 00:02:53,201
The heading tells you how many domains there are in total, so you know whether you are looking at all of them.

41
00:02:53,201 --> 00:02:57,408
And at the bottom, every file the run wrote, collected in one place.

42
00:02:58,008 --> 00:03:02,685
Step five, and if you remember one thing from this video, make it this one.

43
00:03:02,685 --> 00:03:08,858
This is the same tenant and the same time window as before, but with the paging limits turned down.

44
00:03:08,858 --> 00:03:10,729
It reports seventeen messages.

45
00:03:10,729 --> 00:03:12,537
The true answer is fifty two.

46
00:03:12,537 --> 00:03:15,592
The number is not wrong, exactly - it is a floor.

47
00:03:15,592 --> 00:03:18,959
The run stopped before it reached the end of the data.

48
00:03:18,959 --> 00:03:21,204
What makes that safe is the warning.

49
00:03:21,204 --> 00:03:29,248
The run says, in plain language, that the results are incomplete and that the count above is a floor rather than the queue depth.

50
00:03:29,248 --> 00:03:32,179
The older version of this tool did not do that.

51
00:03:32,179 --> 00:03:40,784
It stopped after one page, reported whatever it had, and called itself complete, which is how an under-reported number reaches a customer.

52
00:03:40,784 --> 00:03:45,024
So before you quote a queue depth to anybody, look for that warning.

53
00:03:45,624 --> 00:03:46,154
Step six.

54
00:03:46,154 --> 00:03:52,751
If you are collecting this on a schedule rather than watching it, you do not want to read warnings off a screen.

55
00:03:52,751 --> 00:03:56,049
Pass the pass-through switch and you get an object back.

56
00:03:56,049 --> 00:03:59,819
Check the truncated property before you trust the message count.

57
00:03:59,819 --> 00:04:07,653
If it is true, truncation reason tells you which limit you hit, so you know whether to widen the page limit or narrow the time range.

58
00:04:07,653 --> 00:04:13,896
This is the right way to build a trend: gate on truncated, and only record counts from runs that finished.

59
00:04:14,496 --> 00:04:18,153
Step seven, the zero case, because it will happen to you.

60
00:04:18,153 --> 00:04:20,591
A bare run here reports zero messages.

61
00:04:20,591 --> 00:04:26,430
That is correct, and it is also almost certainly not what the person asking wanted to know.

62
00:04:26,430 --> 00:04:34,386
The tool now explains itself: it names the status filter it actually used, and points out that the default is Pending alone.

63
00:04:34,386 --> 00:04:37,338
This is worth knowing for lab work especially.

64
00:04:37,338 --> 00:04:46,962
Nothing in a test lab is ever genuinely pending, because Exchange treats an unroutable destination as a permanent failure rather than a temporary one.

65
00:04:46,962 --> 00:04:52,865
So synthetic test mail lands as failed, and a bare run will honestly report a queue of zero.

66
00:04:52,865 --> 00:04:55,752
Add failed to the status list and it appears.

67
00:04:56,352 --> 00:04:58,543
Step eight, getting the data out.

68
00:04:58,543 --> 00:05:05,516
Ask for CSV output and you get three files: the full result set, the top senders, and the top recipients.

69
00:05:05,516 --> 00:05:10,032
XML works the same way if you would rather reload the objects later.

70
00:05:10,032 --> 00:05:17,469
Every file the run produced is listed together at the end, so you are not hunting through scrollback for a path.

71
00:05:17,469 --> 00:05:22,848
Use the output path parameter to put them somewhere sensible, like a case folder.

72
00:05:23,448 --> 00:05:27,738
One more thing worth knowing about, particularly in regulated tenants.

73
00:05:27,738 --> 00:05:36,441
Journaling copies every message to an archive address, which can roughly double the delivery count and make a queue look far worse than it is.

74
00:05:36,441 --> 00:05:38,402
There are two switches for this.

75
00:05:38,402 --> 00:05:41,466
Journal-only sizes the journal backlog on its own.

76
00:05:41,466 --> 00:05:44,040
Journal-exclude gives you everything else.

77
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:50,904
The journal address is discovered from the tenant's own journal rules, so you do not need to know it in advance.

78
00:05:51,504 --> 00:05:57,719
Now the limits, because a confident walkthrough that skipped these would do more harm than good.

79
00:05:57,719 --> 00:06:03,870
On premises you can suspend a queue, resume it, force a retry, remove messages, or export them.

80
00:06:03,870 --> 00:06:05,489
None of that exists here.

81
00:06:05,489 --> 00:06:13,387
Exchange Online exposes no queue control plane whatsoever, so this tool reads and reports, and that is all it can ever do.

82
00:06:13,387 --> 00:06:15,135
It only sees outbound flow.

83
00:06:15,135 --> 00:06:20,056
Message trace is not real time, so very recent mail may not have landed yet.

84
00:06:20,056 --> 00:06:27,048
And it remains an approximation built from trace data, which is why every single run prints that disclaimer.

85
00:06:27,648 --> 00:06:28,207
To recap.

86
00:06:28,207 --> 00:06:31,500
Dot-source the file, because running it does nothing.

87
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:36,782
Always pass the status parameter explicitly, so a zero means what you think it means.

88
00:06:36,782 --> 00:06:40,199
Check truncated before you quote any number to anybody.

89
00:06:40,199 --> 00:06:48,028
And read the queue age alongside the depth, because the two together tell you whether you are looking at a burst or an outage.

90
00:06:48,028 --> 00:06:53,309
The current version lives in the team troubleshooting repository, under Get-ExoQueue.

91
00:06:53,309 --> 00:06:54,552
Thanks for watching.

